From c3e8423e114d2e5301bef515f789693341189615 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ElXreno <elxreno@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 21:40:16 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Linux: leave mmap readahead at the kernel default

Every mount zeroes the readahead window of its bdi, so a fault on a
mapped file reads one page and nothing around it. The line came in
with the per filesystem bdi in 2011, "Disable Linux read-ahead handled
by lower layers": read(2) never goes through the page cache, the DMU
prefetcher already streams blocks into the ARC, and the only reader of
the window, readahead into the page cache, filled one page at a time
through zfs_getpage().

With the batch filled from one hold of its dbufs that last reason is
gone, and a mapping pays for every page on its own instead. Starting
clang cold with libLLVM and libclang-cpp mapped from a dataset of 128K
records took 0.35 s with the window at zero and 0.28 s at the kernel
default of 128K, for 9 MiB more read from the disk; medians of three
machines, seven cold starts each. The same window on the page at a time
path was a regression, 0.40 s.

Leave the window as the kernel sets it up, like any other filesystem.
It can still be tuned per mount through
/sys/class/bdi/zfs-*/read_ahead_kb.
---
 module/os/linux/zfs/zfs_vfsops.c | 2 --
 1 file changed, 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/module/os/linux/zfs/zfs_vfsops.c b/module/os/linux/zfs/zfs_vfsops.c
index 2e2e65c..2828b08 100644
--- a/module/os/linux/zfs/zfs_vfsops.c
+++ b/module/os/linux/zfs/zfs_vfsops.c
@@ -1395,8 +1395,6 @@ zfs_domount(struct super_block *sb, const char *osname,
 	if (error)
 		goto out;
 
-	sb->s_bdi->ra_pages = 0;
-
 	/* Set callback operations for the file system. */
 	sb->s_op = &zpl_super_operations;
 	sb->s_xattr = zpl_xattr_handlers;
-- 
2.56.0

